    <!-- Chapter 3: Evidence -->
    <section class="chapter" id="evidence">
      <div class="narrow-container">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 3</div>
        <h2 class="chapter-title">The Evidence Speaks</h2>
        <div class="body-text">
          <p>
            <span class="drop-cap">T</span>he Council's consultation process
            revealed serious flaws in public engagement. The survey contained
            leading questions that appeared designed to guide responses toward
            predetermined outcomes. Shaw Road was positioned at the end of
            lengthy questionnaires where respondents might be less attentive.
            Two separate projects were combined in a way that may have confused
            participants about what they were actually being asked to approve.
          </p>

          <p>
            The initial public outreach was limited in scope. Early opinion
            gathering relied on methods of questionable reliability, including
            anonymous dot-mapping exercises with no verification process.
            Particularly concerning was the collection of feedback from elderly
            residents in care facilities, raising questions about informed
            consent and the validity of responses from individuals who may have
            cognitive impairments.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the North Swanage Traffic Concern Group launched efforts to
            gather more representative public opinion, the Council subsequently
            implemented additional consultation measures. However, by this
            point, much of the planning process had already progressed,
            suggesting decisions may have been made before genuine public input
            was fully considered.
          </p>

          <p>
            These procedural concerns raise important questions about democratic
            accountability and whether the consultation truly reflected
            community views or simply provided a veneer of legitimacy for
            decisions already taken.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4: Solutions -->
    <section class="chapter" id="solutions">
      <div class="narrow-container">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 4</div>
        <h2 class="chapter-title">A Better Way Forward</h2>
        <div class="body-text">
          <p>
            The North Swanage Traffic Concern Group doesn't simply oppose—we
            propose. Our comprehensive alternative plan addresses legitimate
            concerns while preserving essential infrastructure:
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Smart Traffic Management:</strong> Implement dynamic traffic
            controls that adjust to real-time conditions, reducing congestion
            without closures.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Improved Public Transport:</strong> Enhanced bus services
            and park-and-ride facilities to reduce car dependency during peak
            seasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pedestrian Safety Zones:</strong> Designated crossing points
            and traffic calming measures that protect pedestrians without
            severing vital routes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Seasonal Adjustments:</strong> Flexible systems that adapt
            to summer tourism while maintaining year-round accessibility for
            residents.
          </p>

          <p>
            These solutions require investment and imagination, but they offer
            something the closure cannot: a future where Swanage thrives as both
            a destination and a home.
